From 764e26d55288108e482a744796c0df77e281869d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Z User <z@container>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 07:42:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] strings: universal interning (D20) - every ok string is
 canonical

Q ruled the flip the C11 note was waiting for: the interner stops being
a compiler-names-only table and becomes THE canonicalizer - literals,
concat results, conversions, class/method names, host-created values
all settle on one object per content, so 'a' === 'a' is true (=== stays
raw word identity; interning is what makes the words agree).

- the choke point: create_object_string routes through the interner
  (reached via the gc's watch, alloc->gc->interner - live from
  ok_create before any string can exist). the interner's own miss path
  takes the new bare creator create_object_string_uninterned, so there
  is no re-entry. every existing call site is covered with zero
  signature changes.
- the table is now a WEAK set: mark never walks it, sweep removes
  unmarked entries first (string_interner_remove_at, the okmap family
  backward-shift - no tombstones) so a dead canonical can never dangle
  in a probe chain. interned strings live exactly as long as something
  else holds them; the caller rooting discipline is unchanged (fresh
  vs unmarked-table-entry were always the same contract).
- comments updated where the old doctrine was load-bearing: the literal
  site's C11 note, the interner header, native_coro_status's
  immortality claim, the method_map probe note.
- tests: strings/interning.ok + strings/interning_churn.ok; the two
  old-doctrine expectations flipped (classes/identity.ok, and
  loops/fused_conditions.ok's seq-same lane).
- 300k churned strings in 0.55s under a 64mb cap on plain: the table
  pins nothing.
427/427 on plain, threaded, asan, aggressive-gc, asan+aggressive.
